 ],
 "literal": "They have no modesty surely, my eyes, [even when] holding onto modesty / Like the mature pregnancy of a pregnant green snake / The closed sheath of the thick sugarcane opens / Fine drizzle and heavy drops having showered / The cool-coming north wind, to those who have parted, is fire indeed.",
 "translation": "My eyes surely have no modesty. Even when I try to hold onto my modesty, they weep. The cool north wind, which brings fine drizzle and heavy drops that make the closed sheaths of the thick sugarcane—looking like the mature pregnancy of a green snake—burst open, is like fire to those who are separated from their lovers.",
 "modern": "என் கண்கள் நிச்சயமாக நாணமற்றவை. நான் நாணத்தைக் காக்க முயன்றாலும் அவை கண்ணீர் விடுகின்றன. முதிர்ந்த கருவைக் கொண்ட பச்சைப் பாம்பைப் போன்ற, பருத்த கரும்பின் மூடிய மொட்டுகள் அவிழ்ந்து மலருமாறு, நுண்ணிய தூறலையும் பெருந்துளிகளையும் பொழிகின்ற, குளிர்ச்சியாக வீசும் வாடைக் காற்றானது, தம் காதலரைப் பிரிந்திருப்பவருக்கு நெருப்பைப் போன்றதாகும்.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her companion in deep distress during the cold season. Her lover has not returned as promised, and she is struggling to hide her grief. She laments that her eyes have lost all modesty because they weep openly, revealing her secret pain to everyone. The poem features a vivid image of sugarcane sheaths, swollen like a pregnant green snake, bursting open under the cold rain. This natural image mirrors the heroine's own state, where her tightly guarded secret of separation is forced open by the cold north wind, which feels not cool, but like a burning fire to her.",
